course: dictionary
lesson: 1283
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. «Butter» can be a food, or a word for spreading that food on something. The words around it give you clues. In «Shake cream in a jar long enough and the droplets stick together into butter», butter is the food that forms, so it is the soft, yellow food.
explain: The word next to it can change the meaning. «Peanut butter» is a smooth spread made from crushed nuts, not a dairy food. Phrases such as «bread and butter» and «butter up» mean something different from the single words. To butter up someone means to flatter them, so it is not about spreading anything at all.
explain: Do not trust a myth just because someone says it. «A boy in Leeds believed the myth that you should put butter on a burn; he did, and the burn blistered badly until he cooled it with water at the doctor's.» The clue is the ending: the burn got worse, so butter was not the answer.
fact: Butter is a soft, yellow food made from cream.
fact: To butter something means to spread butter on it, so «butter» can also be a verb.
fact: Butter is made by churning cream, which means shaking or stirring it until the fat sticks together.
fact: Butter melts when warm and goes hard in the fridge.
fact: For a burn, cool running water for 20 minutes is what helps. Putting butter on a burn is a myth, and it does not cool the burn.
exercise: «Shake cream in a jar long enough and the droplets stick together into butter.» In this sentence, what is butter? | A word for shaking | *A soft, yellow food made from cream | To spread something on bread | A kind of jar
exercise: «Peanut butter works better in humane traps.» What does peanut butter mean? | A cream cake | Nuts that have been shaken in a jar | *A smooth spread made from crushed nuts | A kind of bread
exercise: «Lena buttered her toast before school.» What does buttered mean? | *Spread with butter | Cooked in a hot pan | Cut into thin slices | Shaken hard in a jar
test: In «bread and butter», what is butter? | Cream shaken until it turns hard | A kind of bread | Flattering someone | *The soft, yellow food made from cream
test: «Don't butter me up with compliments, Dad said.» What does butter up mean? | To melt in the sun | To spread butter on someone | *To flatter someone to get what you want | To drop things by accident
exam: Which is true about butter? | It is a cold, blue liquid | It is made by baking flour | It is a kind of cheese that is blue | *It melts when warm and goes hard in the fridge
